== Title decisions ==

* '''[[Planning]] → [[Planning (artificial intelligence)]]''' because planning is also a durable human, organizational, military, and project-management concept.
* '''[[Tool use]] → [[Tool use (artificial intelligence)]]''' because tool use is also a major concept in human cognition, animal behavior, anthropology, and robotics.
* '''[[Agent memory]] → [[Agent memory (artificial intelligence)]]''' because memory has established biological/cognitive meanings and “agent memory” is now used for multiple computational architectures.
* '''[[Autonomous agent]] → [[Autonomous agent (artificial intelligence)]]''' to distinguish computational autonomy from ordinary legal, organizational, or human agency.
* [[Artificial intelligence]], [[Intelligent agent]], [[AI agent]], [[Multi-agent system]], and [[Large language model]] remain unqualified.
* [[AI agent]] is retained separately from [[Intelligent agent]], but only as a modern applied AI-system concept rather than a duplicate name for the classical abstraction.

== Domain decision ==

No new controlled Domain is required.

Primary Domains are existing '''Computer science''', with Systems science, Mathematics, Cognitive science, Linguistics, and Philosophy used only where substantively justified.

No “Artificial intelligence” Domain is introduced.

== Core distinction: intelligent agent versus AI agent ==

[[Intelligent agent]] is the established general AI abstraction:

<pre>
perceive environment
→ select action
→ act toward objective
</pre>

Its classical treatment includes software agents, robots, game agents, control systems, and other computational actors.

[[AI agent]] is retained as the contemporary applied system term for AI-powered systems that operate over multi-step tasks, often using foundation models, planning, tools, external state, and feedback loops.

The relationship is:

<pre>
AI agent ⊆ intelligent-agent style systems (practical contemporary usage)
intelligent agent ≠ necessarily LLM-based
AI agent ≠ necessarily fully autonomous
AI agent ≠ chatbot by definition
</pre>

Terminology around “AI agent”, “agentic AI”, and “autonomous agent” is still evolving. NIST's 2026 agentic-AI work and current academic surveys support the reality of the field while also showing taxonomy fragmentation.

