== Normalized concept dossiers ==

=== Artificial intelligence ===
Artificial intelligence is the field concerned with computational systems that perform tasks involving perception, reasoning, learning, planning, language, decision-making, action, or other capabilities associated with intelligent behavior.

The field includes symbolic, statistical, machine-learning, search, planning, optimization, knowledge-representation, robotics, and hybrid approaches.

'''Do not conflate:''' AI ≠ machine learning; AI ≠ generative AI; AI ≠ human-level intelligence.

=== Intelligent agent ===
An intelligent agent is a computational entity modeled as perceiving an environment and selecting actions to pursue objectives.

Classical agent theory emphasizes environment, percepts, actions, goals/utility, rationality, autonomy, and interaction.

'''Do not conflate:''' an intelligent agent need not use an LLM, natural language, tools, or human-like cognition.

=== AI agent ===
An AI agent is a contemporary AI-powered software/system entity designed to carry out goal-directed multi-step tasks by selecting and executing actions, often with environmental feedback.

Modern AI agents frequently integrate foundation models or LLMs with planning, tools, memory/state, retrieval, and execution systems.

The exact architecture is not standardized.

'''Do not conflate:''' model ≠ agent; chatbot ≠ agent; tool-calling model ≠ automatically a full agent.

=== Planning (artificial intelligence) ===
AI planning is the computational problem of selecting or synthesizing actions that can move an agent/system from an initial situation toward a goal under an action/environment model.

Classical automated planning is model-based and distinct from execution; planning-and-acting literature studies integration with ongoing action and feedback.

'''Do not conflate:''' planning ≠ pathfinding only; planning ≠ chain-of-thought text.

=== Tool use (artificial intelligence) ===
AI tool use is the ability of a model or agent system to select, invoke, and incorporate external functions/resources such as APIs, calculators, databases, search systems, code execution, software interfaces, or devices.

Tool-learning research distinguishes tool selection, argument generation, invocation, result interpretation, and multi-tool orchestration.

'''Do not conflate:''' tool use ≠ one function-call API; tool use does not itself establish autonomous agency.

=== Agent memory (artificial intelligence) ===
Agent memory is persistent or selectively retained computational state that enables an artificial agent to use information from prior observations, interactions, actions, or learned experience in future decision-making.

Modern memory can be token/context resident, external/retrieved, parametric, latent, episodic-like, factual, experiential, or working-state-like depending on architecture.

'''Do not conflate:''' agent memory ≠ context window; ≠ vector database; ≠ RAG; ≠ biological memory.

=== Multi-agent system ===
A multi-agent system is a computational system containing multiple interacting agents whose behaviors can be cooperative, competitive, mixed-motive, coordinated, decentralized, or partially independent.

The field predates LLMs and draws on distributed AI, game theory, economics, control, robotics, and distributed systems.

'''Do not conflate:''' multiple LLM calls ≠ multi-agent system by definition; multi-agent system ≠ distributed system generally.

=== Autonomous agent (artificial intelligence) ===
An autonomous AI agent is an artificial agent that can select and execute actions over time with limited immediate external control, using its own policy/deliberation and environmental feedback within defined objectives and constraints.

Autonomy is a degree, not an all-or-nothing property.

'''Do not conflate:''' autonomous ≠ unconstrained; autonomous ≠ unsupervised; autonomous ≠ safe/reliable.

=== Large language model ===
A large language model is a large-scale statistical/neural model trained to model and generate language or token sequences, usually through self-supervised learning over very large corpora and often adapted for downstream tasks.

Modern LLMs are commonly transformer-based, but “LLM” describes the language-model class/scale/use rather than a theorem requiring one exact architecture.

'''Do not conflate:''' LLM ≠ chatbot; LLM ≠ agent; LLM ≠ foundation model as a strict synonym.

== Modern evidence corrections ==

=== Agent taxonomy fragmentation ===
Current 2025–2026 surveys repeatedly note inconsistent terminology around AI agents, autonomous agents, agentic AI, planning, tools, and memory.

The Core pages must therefore distinguish:

<pre>
classical intelligent-agent abstraction
modern AI-agent implementation pattern
agentic AI as broader architectural/research framing
autonomy as a property/degree
</pre>

rather than retrofitting all classical agent theory into an LLM-agent taxonomy.

=== NIST 2026 agentic AI ===
NIST now treats agentic AI as a distinct standards/evaluation/risk-management area and describes such systems as autonomous agents capable of decision-making, learning/adaptation, goal-directed behavior, and interaction with users/systems/environments.

Use this as evidence of current institutional terminology, not as the sole definition of all AI agents.

=== Planning ===
Ghallab, Nau & Traverso distinguish planning from acting.

A plan can be synthesized before execution, but real actors may need continual planning, monitoring, replanning, and other deliberation during execution.

LLM-agent “planning” must not silently replace the older automated-planning concept.

=== Tool use ===
Current tool-learning surveys describe a pipeline including tool retrieval/selection, planning, invocation/execution, and response/result integration.

Function calling is one implementation mechanism, not the entire concept.

Recent 2026 work emphasizes long-horizon multi-tool orchestration, state, feedback, safety, cost, and verifiability beyond one-shot tool calls.

=== Agent memory ===
Current 2025–2026 agent-memory surveys explicitly distinguish agent memory from:

* LLM internal/parametric memory;
* context engineering;
* retrieval-augmented generation;
* simple vector stores;
* human memory taxonomies.

The page should treat “short-term / long-term” analogies as optional descriptive metaphors, not universal architecture.

=== Multi-agent systems ===
Wooldridge's standard treatment predates modern LLM agents and defines MAS around multiple interacting autonomous computational agents.

The new wave must preserve that historical field rather than define MAS as “a team of LLM personas”.

=== Large language models ===
LLMs are a subset of language models and often a subset of foundation models in contemporary practice, but “foundation model” and “LLM” emphasize different properties.

A language model can be large without being an agent; an agent can use an LLM without being identical to it.

